WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:05.048
When a replay has a long list of failed
checks, the dashboard assistant can help

00:00:05.048 --> 00:00:07.548
you pick the first request to investigate.

00:00:08.085 --> 00:00:11.149
Open the replay report, then click the chat
icon.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.612
The assistant already has this report in

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:16.764
scope, so you can ask about the current run
right away.

00:00:17.319 --> 00:00:19.396
Start with, What changed in this replay?

00:00:19.566 --> 00:00:21.395
Which endpoint should I check first?

00:00:21.566 --> 00:00:24.531
If you already know the endpoint, name it
in the prompt.

00:00:24.702 --> 00:00:28.218
The example here asks why assertions failed
for /order.

00:00:28.785 --> 00:00:33.844
In the example, the assistant groups the
failures by assertion type for /order.

00:00:34.015 --> 00:00:36.700
Both JSON schema and body checks failed.

00:00:36.871 --> 00:00:39.800
Use those details to choose a request to
inspect.

00:00:40.352 --> 00:00:41.385
Ask a follow-up.

00:00:41.556 --> 00:00:45.588
Show me one /order request with a failed
JSON body assertion.

00:00:45.758 --> 00:00:50.620
Ask for the request link, or filter the
report’s Accuracy tab by endpoint and

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.830
assertion type to find it yourself.

00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:56.000
Open the affected request in the report and

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.675
compare the recorded and replayed
responses.

00:00:58.846 --> 00:01:02.439
This separate example shows where to find
Expected and Actual.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.710
Look for the difference the assertion
flagged.

00:01:05.252 --> 00:01:08.228
Try this on your next replay with a failed
assertion.
